# What happens when prices are not allowed to move?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Queues, black markets, rationing: what replaces the price? What did empty Soviet shelves and wartime ration books have in common?"

## Two bare shelves
sources:
    - id: iwm
      title: What you need to know about rationing in the Second World War
      authors: Imperial War Museums
      year: 2020
      finding: British food rationing began on 8 January 1940 with bacon, butter and sugar; meat and many other foods followed. Bread, not rationed during the war, was rationed from July 1946.
      url: https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/what-you-need-to-know-about-rationing-in-the-second-world-war
    - id: bodleian1954
      title: 'On this day in 1954: Food rationing ends after 14 years'
      authors: Bodleian Libraries, University of Oxford
      year: 2011
      finding: Food rationing in Britain ended at midnight on 3/4 July 1954, when meat and bacon came off the ration.
      url: https://blogs.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/archivesandmanuscripts/2011/07/04/on-this-day-in-1954-food-rationing-ends-after-14-years/
    - id: kornai1980
      title: Economics of Shortage
      authors: János Kornai
      year: 1980
      finding: 'Chronic shortage in the socialist economies of Central and Eastern Europe was systemic: queues for basic goods, waiting lists for durables, forced substitution and forced saving.'
    - id: csm1991
      title: Prices Take a Hike in Soviet Capital
      authors: The Christian Science Monitor
      year: 1991
      finding: Soviet state price rises took effect on 2 April 1991; some food items, such as meat and bread, tripled in price.
      url: https://www.csmonitor.com/1991/0403/03031.html

